WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:11.011 --> 00:00:13.847
- There haven't been any full-blooded Filipino

00:00:13.847 --> 00:00:16.766
that has been to the NBA, and I just wanna be the

00:00:16.766 --> 00:00:19.477
first one, and I just wanna show everyone that we

00:00:19.477 --> 00:00:20.437
can also make it.

00:00:27.736 --> 00:00:31.322
My dad always told me to watch films 
of Hakeem Olajuwon,

00:00:31.322 --> 00:00:34.034
Tim Duncan, those guys, and I just kept

00:00:34.034 --> 00:00:35.660
watching, you know, every day.

00:00:36.578 --> 00:00:38.830
My dad was in the professional leagues in the

00:00:38.830 --> 00:00:39.456
Philippines.

00:00:39.456 --> 00:00:42.125
So, you know, he just taught me almost everything

00:00:42.125 --> 00:00:43.460
he knows about the game.

00:00:43.460 --> 00:00:45.170
And we always started with footwork.

00:00:45.170 --> 00:00:47.630
You know, how to make easy shots.

00:00:47.630 --> 00:00:50.133
- You know, you hear 7'0", and sometimes that

00:00:50.133 --> 00:00:53.595
means 6'10", 6'11". He's legit 7'2"

00:00:53.595 --> 00:00:55.930
So, it's just a breath of fresh air to work with

00:00:55.930 --> 00:00:59.059
somebody whose goals are so in reach but also

00:00:59.059 --> 00:01:01.644
knows and is willing to put the work in 
that it's gonna

00:01:01.644 --> 00:01:02.729
take to get there.

00:01:02.729 --> 00:01:05.148
- Basketball is really a religion to the Philippines.

00:01:05.148 --> 00:01:07.400
And, uh, every time you go out of your house, you'll

00:01:07.400 --> 00:01:09.235
see a court here, court there.

00:01:09.235 --> 00:01:10.945
You'll just see everyone playing basketball,

00:01:10.945 --> 00:01:14.407
and that's just how we love the, the sport 
in our country.

00:01:14.407 --> 00:01:16.076
- He's definitely, like, a type of basketball player

00:01:16.076 --> 00:01:16.701
that'll pass.

00:01:16.701 --> 00:01:17.786
Like, he'll, he'll look for you.

00:01:17.786 --> 00:01:19.245
He's not a selfish player.

00:01:21.164 --> 00:01:23.625
- You know, the mentality in the States 
when you're a

00:01:23.625 --> 00:01:27.545
foreign player with hype and accolades coming in,

00:01:27.545 --> 00:01:28.880
people wanna play against you.

00:01:28.880 --> 00:01:30.799
You know, everybody has their goals, 
and everybody's

00:01:30.799 --> 00:01:31.382
trying to get on.

00:01:31.382 --> 00:01:34.344
So what better way to get recruited to a college

00:01:34.344 --> 00:01:36.846
than saying, "Hey, I had X amount of points and

00:01:36.846 --> 00:01:39.140
rebounds and blocks against Kai Sotto 
and here's my

00:01:39.140 --> 00:01:40.183
film against him"?

00:01:40.183 --> 00:01:42.602
I think he realizes that now, and he kinda brings

00:01:42.602 --> 00:01:44.729
it to his opponents instead of waiting 
for it to come to him.

00:01:44.729 --> 00:01:46.856
- Yes, sir. Yes, sir.

00:01:46.856 --> 00:01:49.317
The first few months, I'm just getting used to, like,

00:01:49.317 --> 00:01:53.613
the, like, the culture, the people and, uh, the

00:01:53.613 --> 00:01:54.572
basketball here.

00:01:54.572 --> 00:01:58.034
And, uh, I just, I'm just very happy that I have my

00:01:58.034 --> 00:01:59.035
whole family here.

00:01:59.035 --> 00:02:01.287
I didn't really know a lot of things about college

00:02:01.287 --> 00:02:04.791
recruitment cuz, uh, in the Philippines, I was still

00:02:04.791 --> 00:02:06.167
at the 10th grade.

00:02:06.167 --> 00:02:08.837
So, I wasn't really, like, focused on any college, 
but when

00:02:08.837 --> 00:02:11.589
I came here, you know, I, I just got a little shocked

00:02:11.589 --> 00:02:14.801
that things are happening so fast and, uh, I just

00:02:14.801 --> 00:02:15.760
have to be ready for it.

00:02:18.138 --> 00:02:21.683
- I see Kai as somebody with a world 
of potential, um,

00:02:21.683 --> 00:02:24.144
and, and the sky's really the limit, but also, you

00:02:24.144 --> 00:02:25.770
know, there's a short time to get there, and it

00:02:25.770 --> 00:02:29.399
has to be deliberate and making 
those goals achievable.

00:02:31.276 --> 00:02:33.820
- The training here in the States is different

00:02:33.820 --> 00:02:36.656
because they're really, like, focused, and my trainer

00:02:36.656 --> 00:02:39.868
really dedicated themselves to help me get better

00:02:39.868 --> 00:02:40.577
as a player.

00:02:41.619 --> 00:02:43.872
- One thing that we wanted to really 
work on, getting

00:02:43.872 --> 00:02:47.083
him to be more NBA-ready, um, which means, you

00:02:47.083 --> 00:02:49.627
know, can he handle the NBA, uh, 
pace of the game,

00:02:49.627 --> 00:02:50.753
physicality.

00:02:50.753 --> 00:02:53.882
Um, can he use this link, uh, in a meaningful way?

00:02:53.882 --> 00:02:56.384
Does he stretch the floor consistently?

00:02:56.384 --> 00:02:57.802
Um, can he guard players?

00:02:57.802 --> 00:02:59.888
Can he guard in the post? 
Can he guard off switches?

00:03:06.269 --> 00:03:09.189
- Coach Chuck Person, Coach Rob, Jeremiah.

00:03:09.189 --> 00:03:10.857
They've been helping me a lot.

00:03:10.857 --> 00:03:11.858
They're helping me prepare.

00:03:11.858 --> 00:03:15.945
And, uh, I just, I just get my confidence from my

00:03:15.945 --> 00:03:17.363
workouts, from my practices.

00:03:17.363 --> 00:03:19.115
- I think for him to become,

00:03:19.115 --> 00:03:20.617
become more physical,

00:03:20.617 --> 00:03:22.702
a lot of it's gonna take some work at P3,

00:03:22.702 --> 00:03:24.078
which he's working at every day.

00:03:24.078 --> 00:03:26.372
They do a great job, um, kind of sports science

00:03:26.372 --> 00:03:28.833
and, and strength and conditioning to develop him

00:03:28.833 --> 00:03:31.461
off the court, which I think is really, really important.

00:03:31.461 --> 00:03:33.671
- Here at P3, we assess our athletes so that 
we know

00:03:33.671 --> 00:03:34.547
what the needs are.

00:03:34.547 --> 00:03:38.259
So with Kai, I think from, from this point on, it's

00:03:38.259 --> 00:03:40.261
just a matter of continuing to develop his strength,

00:03:40.261 --> 00:03:42.472
develop that power. He's gonna be playing and

00:03:42.472 --> 00:03:44.224
matching up against guys who are bigger than him.

00:03:44.224 --> 00:03:46.476
So making sure that he is able to match that

00:03:46.476 --> 00:03:48.770
intensity on the court, but also making sure that

00:03:48.770 --> 00:03:51.105
he stays healthy too to be able to 
compete year-round.

00:03:53.316 --> 00:03:55.109
- That was a moment in time for him at

00:03:55.109 --> 00:03:56.069
Basketball Without Borders,

00:03:56.069 --> 00:03:58.655
where all 30 NBA teams are there.

00:03:58.655 --> 00:04:00.823
There's high-level kids that are his age that are

00:04:00.823 --> 00:04:03.159
there, and he's able to see, you know, "I didn't

00:04:03.159 --> 00:04:05.745
make the, the first team. I wasn't MVP. I wasn't on

00:04:05.745 --> 00:04:07.789
the first-team all-camp.

00:04:07.789 --> 00:04:09.123
Um, and so what are the things

00:04:09.123 --> 00:04:10.792
I need to do to improve?"

00:04:10.792 --> 00:04:13.002
And so now you're able to look at positive feedback

00:04:13.002 --> 00:04:14.671
from GMs, scouts.

00:04:14.671 --> 00:04:15.755
- Uh, it was great.

00:04:15.755 --> 00:04:18.800
Uh, I had to—I had the opportunity to play against

00:04:18.800 --> 00:04:21.970
the best players all over the world and, uh, to be

00:04:21.970 --> 00:04:23.429
coached by great coaches.

00:04:24.722 --> 00:04:26.766
- What are the gaps? What's gonna help 
him get there?

00:04:26.766 --> 00:04:28.643
And I think he took it to heart.

00:04:28.643 --> 00:04:30.979
You know, I think he saw kind of where he was and

00:04:30.979 --> 00:04:32.730
was able to size himself up a little bit.

00:04:32.730 --> 00:04:35.275
And now he's able to take a step forward because

00:04:35.275 --> 00:04:36.859
he's, he's grounded in reality.

00:04:36.859 --> 00:04:38.987
He's not caught kind of in the hype world.

00:04:38.987 --> 00:04:40.613
You know, potential is something people

00:04:40.613 --> 00:04:41.447
talk about a lot, right?

00:04:41.447 --> 00:04:42.615
Potential, potential, potential.

00:04:42.615 --> 00:04:44.659
There's always a point, though, where you have to

00:04:44.659 --> 00:04:47.870
start turning a corner, and potential has 
to become reality.

00:04:47.870 --> 00:04:50.999
And for him, he's starting to, to approach that phase.

00:04:50.999 --> 00:04:52.166
And I think he understands that.

00:04:52.166 --> 00:04:54.752
And so as long as he continues to 
move forward and

00:04:54.752 --> 00:04:56.796
progress, he's got every, every opportunity in the

00:04:56.796 --> 00:04:58.298
world to be a good NBA player.

00:05:00.758 --> 00:05:03.136
- My ultimate dream is to make it to the NBA.

00:05:03.136 --> 00:05:06.055
And I also want to represent the Philippines on

00:05:06.055 --> 00:05:07.390
international tournaments.

00:05:09.726 --> 00:05:12.353
I just want to show that Philippines is part of the

00:05:12.353 --> 00:05:13.980
basketball world.

00:05:15.231 --> 00:05:18.026
I appreciate those people who follow me and my

00:05:18.026 --> 00:05:21.988
journey, knowing that all my fellow Filipinos from

00:05:21.988 --> 00:05:24.699
my country, you know, supporting me and, uh,

00:05:24.699 --> 00:05:28.536
following me, and I just use it as a motivation,

00:05:28.536 --> 00:05:32.498
inspiration, just to work even harder to 
make them proud.

